times, to have almost entirely lost control. The hero, the wonderful young

7
00:00:36,479 --> 00:00:41,439
Parisian in whom the romantic and the scientific temperaments were so strangely

8
00:00:41,439 --> 00:00:48,957
blended, became to him a kind of prefiguring type of himself and And, indeed, the whole book

9
00:00:48,957 --> 00:00:54,599
seemed to him to contain the story of his own life, written before he had lived

10
00:00:54,599 --> 00:01:00,599
it. In one point he was more fortunate than the novel’s fantastic hero. He never

11
00:01:00,599 --> 00:01:06,680
knew— —never, indeed, had any cause to know— —that somewhat grotesque dread of mirrors,

12
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:11,680
and polished metal surfaces, and still water which came upon the young Parisian

13
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:16,637
so early in his life, and was occasioned by the sudden decay of a bow that had

14
00:01:16,637 --> 00:01:19,759
once appar apparently, been so remarkable.

15
00:01:19,759 --> 00:01:25,000
It was with an almost cruel joy— —and perhaps in nearly every joy, as certainly

16
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,319
in every pleasure, cruelty has its place— —that he used to read the latter part of

17
00:01:30,319 --> 00:01:35,957
the book, with its really tragic, if somewhat overemphasized, account of the sorrow and

18
00:01:35,957 --> 00:01:41,759
despair of one who had himself lost what in others, and the world, he had most dearly

19
00:01:41,759 --> 00:01:47,400
valued. For the wonderful beauty that had so fascinated Basil hallwood and many

20
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:53,080
others besides him, seemed never to leave him. Even those who had heard the most

21
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:57,479
evil things against him— —and from time to time strange rumors about his mode of

22
00:01:57,479 --> 00:02:01,878
life crept through London and became the chatter of the clubs— —could not believe

23
00:02:01,878 --> 00:02:06,637
anything to his dishonor when they saw him. He had always the look of one who

24
00:02:06,637 --> 00:02:11,439
had kept himself unspotted from the world. Men who talked grossly became

25
00:02:11,439 --> 00:02:15,159
silent when Dorian Gray entered the room.

26
00:02:15,159 --> 00:02:20,040
There was something in the purity of his face that rebuked them. His mere presence

27
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:24,479
seemed to recall to them the memory of the innocence that they had

28
00:02:24,479 --> 00:02:28,797
tarnished. They wondered how one so charming and graceful as he was could

29
00:02:28,797 --> 00:02:33,119
have escaped the stain of an age that was at once sorted and

30
00:02:33,119 --> 00:02:37,957
sensual. Often, on returning home from one of those mysterious and prolonged

31
00:02:37,957 --> 00:02:43,000
absences that gave rise to such strange conjecture among those who were his

32
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,919
friends, or thought that they were so, he himself would creep up upstairs to the

33
00:02:47,919 --> 00:02:53,680
locked room, open the door with the key that never left him now, and stand, with a

34
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:58,119
mirror, in front of the portrait that Basil hallwood had painted of him,

35
00:02:58,119 --> 00:03:03,319
looking now at the evil and aging face on the canvas, and now at the fair young

36
00:03:03,319 --> 00:03:08,000
face that laughed back at him from the polished glass. The very sharpness of the

37
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,680
contrast used to quicken his sense of pleasure. He grew more and more enamored

38
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:16,240
of his own beauty, more and more interested in the corruption of his own

39
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:21,637
soul. He would examine with minute care, and sometimes with a monstrous and

40
00:03:21,637 --> 00:03:26,957
terrible delight, the hideous lines that seared the wrinkling forehead or crawled

41
00:03:26,957 --> 00:03:32,759
around the heavy sensual mouth, wondering sometimes which were the more horrible,

42
00:03:32,759 --> 00:03:38,718
the signs of sin or the signs of age. He would place his white hands

43
00:03:38,718 --> 00:03:45,520
beside the coarse bloated hands of the picture, and smile. He mocked the misshapen body and

44
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:47,599
the failing limbs.

45
00:03:47,599 --> 00:03:52,919
There were moments, indeed, at night, when, lying sleepless in his own delicately

46
00:03:52,919 --> 00:03:57,637
scented chamber, or in the sorted room of the little ill-fame tavern near the

47
00:03:57,637 --> 00:04:03,637
docks which, under an assumed name and in disguise, it was his habit to frequent, he

48
00:04:03,637 --> 00:04:07,400
would think of the ruin he had brought upon his soul with a pity that was all

49
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:12,759
the more poignant because it was purely selfish. But moments such as these were

50
00:04:12,759 --> 00:04:17,560
rare. That curiosity about life which Lord Henry had first stirred in him him,

51
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:22,360
as they sat together in the garden of their friend, seemed to increase with

52
00:04:22,360 --> 00:04:27,400
gratification. The more he knew, the more he desired to know. He had mad hungers

53
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:33,000
that grew more ravenous as he fed them. Yet he was not really reckless, at

54
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,319
any rate in his relations to society. Once or twice every month during

55
00:04:38,319 --> 00:04:43,680
the winter, and on each Wednesday evening while the season lasted, he would throw

56
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:48,400
open to the world his beautiful house and have the most celebrated musicians

57
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:53,479
of the day to charm his guests with the wonders of their art. His little dinners,

58
00:04:53,479 --> 00:04:57,439
in the settling of which Lord Henry always assisted him, were noted as much

59
00:04:57,439 --> 00:05:02,680
for the careful selection and placing of those invited, as for the exquisite taste

60
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:07,560
shown in the decoration of the table, with its subtle symphonic arrangements

61
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:14,080
of exotic flowers, and embroidered cloths, and antique plate of gold and

62
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:20,040
silver. Indeed, there were many, especially among the very young men, who

63
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:25,637
saw, or fancied that they saw, in Dorian Gray the true realization of a type of

64
00:05:25,637 --> 00:05:31,000
which they had often dreamed in Eaton or Oxford days, a type that was to combine

65
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,319
something of the real culture of the scholar with all the grace and

66
00:05:34,319 --> 00:05:39,718
distinction and perfect manner of a citizen of the world. To them he seemed to be of the

67
00:05:39,718 --> 00:05:44,797
company of those whom Dante describes as having sought to “make themselves perfect

68
00:05:44,797 --> 00:05:52,040
by the worship of beauty.” Like gotier he was one for whom “the visible world

69
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:58,080
existed.” And, certainly, to him life itself was the first, the greatest, of the arts,

70
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:04,479
and for it all the other arts seemed to be but a preparation. Fashion, by which what is

71
00:06:04,479 --> 00:06:10,240
really fantastic becomes for a moment universal, and dandism which, in its own

72
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:15,479
way, is an attempt to assert the absolute modernity of beauty, had, of course, their

73
00:06:15,479 --> 00:06:20,159
fascination for him. His mode of dressing, and the particular styles that from time

74
00:06:20,159 --> 00:06:24,520
to time he affected, had their marked influence on the young exquisites of the

75
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:30,119
Mayfair balls and palm club windows, who copied him in everything that he did, and

76
00:06:30,119 --> 00:06:37,119
tried to reproduce the accidental charm of his graceful, though to him only half serious

77
00:06:37,119 --> 00:06:41,479
feries For, while he was but too ready to accept the position that was almost

78
00:06:41,479 --> 00:06:46,479
immediately offered to him on his coming of age, and found, indeed, a subtle

79
00:06:46,479 --> 00:06:50,159
pleasure in the thought that he might really become to the London of his own

80
00:06:50,159 --> 00:06:55,919
day what to imperial Neronian Rome the author of the satiric con once had

81
00:06:55,919 --> 00:07:01,000
been, yet in his inmost heart he desired to be something more than a mere arbiter

82
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,319
elegantiarum to be consulted on the wearing of a jewel, or the knotting of a

83
00:07:06,319 --> 00:07:12,279
necktie, or the conduct of a cane. He sought to elaborate some new scheme of

84
00:07:12,279 --> 00:07:17,718
life that would have its reasoned philosophy and its ordered principles, and find in the

85
00:07:17,718 --> 00:07:24,159
spiritualizing of the senses its highest realization. The worship of the senses

86
00:07:24,159 --> 00:07:30,119
has often, and with much justice, been decried, men feeling a natural instinct

87
00:07:30,119 --> 00:07:35,718
of terror about passions and sensations that seem stronger than themselves, and

88
00:07:35,718 --> 00:07:40,797
that they are conscious of sharing with the less highly organized forms of

89
00:07:40,797 --> 00:07:45,360
existence. But it appeared to Dorian Gray that the true nature of the senses had

90
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:50,000
never been understood, and that they had remained savage an animal merely because

91
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,159
the world had sought to starve them into submission or to kill them by pain,

92
00:07:55,159 --> 00:07:59,080
instead of aiming at making them elements of a new spirituality, of which

93
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:05,000
a fine instinct for beauty was to be the dominant characteristic. As he looked back upon

94
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:11,040
man moving through history, he was haunted by a feeling of loss. So much had

95
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:17,558
been surrendered! and to such little purpose! There had been mad willful rejections,

96
00:08:17,558 --> 00:08:22,797
monstrous forms of self-torture and self-denial, whose origin was fear and

97
00:08:22,797 --> 00:08:26,759
whose result was a degradation infinitely more terrible than that fancy

98
00:08:26,759 --> 00:08:32,040
degradation from which, in their ignorance, they had sought to escape;

99
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:37,080
Nature, in her wonderful irony, driving out the anchorite to feed with the wild

100
00:08:37,080 --> 00:08:43,000
animals of the desert and giving to the hermit the beasts of the field as his

101
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:49,400
companions. Yes: there was to be, as Lord Henry had had prophesied, a new Hedonism

102
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:54,797
that was to recreate life and to save it from that harsh uncomely puritanism that

103
00:08:54,797 --> 00:09:00,797
is having, in our own day, its curious revival. It was to have its service of

104
00:09:00,797 --> 00:09:05,119
the intellect, certainly, yet it was never to accept any theory or system that

105
00:09:05,119 --> 00:09:11,520
would involve the sacrifice of any mode of passionate experience. Its aim, indeed, was to be

106
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:17,000
experience itself, and not the fruits of experience, sweet or bitter as they might

107
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:22,759
be. Of the asceticism that deadens the senses, as of the vulgar profligacy that

108
00:09:22,759 --> 00:09:28,240
dulls them, it was to know nothing. But it was to teach man to concentrate himself

109
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:32,520
upon the moments of a life that is itself but a

110
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:38,319
moment. There are few of us who have not sometimes wakened before dawn, either

111
00:09:38,319 --> 00:09:43,360
after one of those dreamless nights that make us almost enamored of death, or one

112
00:09:43,360 --> 00:09:47,919
of those nights of horror and misshapen joy, when through the chambers of the

113
00:09:47,919 --> 00:09:54,279
brain sweep phantoms more terrible than reality itself, and instinct with that

114
00:09:54,279 --> 00:09:59,360
vivid life that lurks in all grotesques, and that lends to Gothic art its

115
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:05,599
enduring vitality, this art being, one might fancy, especially the art of those

116
00:10:05,599 --> 00:10:10,878
whose minds have been troubled with the malady of revery Gradually white fingers

117
00:10:10,878 --> 00:10:15,759
creep through the curtains, and they appear to tremble. In black fantastic

118
00:10:15,759 --> 00:10:21,080
shapes, dumb shadows crawl into the corners of the room and crouch

119
00:10:21,080 --> 00:10:26,840
there. Outside, there is the stirring of birds among the leaves, or the sound of

120
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:31,759
men going forth to their work, or the sigh and sob of the wind coming down

121
00:10:31,759 --> 00:10:37,240
from the hills and wandering around the silent house, as though it feared to wake

122
00:10:37,240 --> 00:10:43,000
the sleepers and yet must needs call forth sleep from her purple cave. Veil

123
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:48,240
after veil of thin dusky gwes is lifted, and by degrees the forms and colors of

124
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:53,000
things are restored to them, and we watch the dawn remaking the world in its

125
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:59,200
antique pattern. The one mirrors get back their mimic life. The flameless tapers

126
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:04,159
stand where we had left them, and beside them lies the half cut book that we had

127
00:11:04,159 --> 00:11:09,000
been studying, or the wired flower that we had worn at the ball, or the letter

128
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:15,119
that we had been afraid to read, or that we had read too often. Nothing seems to us

129
00:11:15,119 --> 00:11:20,919
changed. Out of the unreal shadows of the night comes back the real life that we had

130
00:11:20,919 --> 00:11:26,240
known. We have to resume it where we had left off, and there steals over us a

131
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:30,637
terrible sense of the necessity for the continuance of energy in the same

132
00:11:30,637 --> 00:11:37,000
wearisome round of stereotyped habits, or a wild longing, it may be, that our

133
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,479
eyelids might open some morning upon a world that had been refashioned a new in

134
00:11:41,479 --> 00:11:46,080
the darkness for our pleasure, a world in which things would have fresh shapes and

135
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:51,360
colors and be changed, or have other secrets, a world in which the past would

136
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:56,520
have little or no place, or survive, at any rate, in no conscious form of

137
00:11:56,520 --> 00:12:02,558
obligation or regret, the remembrance even of joy having its bitterness and

138
00:12:02,558 --> 00:12:07,759
the memories of pleasure their pain. It was the creation of such worlds

139
00:12:07,759 --> 00:12:13,479
as these that seemed to Dorian Gray to be the true object, or amongst the true

140
00:12:13,479 --> 00:12:19,080
objects, of life; and in his search for sensations that would be at once new and

141
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:23,878
delightful, and possess that element of strangeness that is so essential to

142
00:12:23,878 --> 00:12:28,520
romance, he would often adopt certain modes of thought that he knew to be

143
00:12:28,520 --> 00:12:34,200
really alien to his nature, abandon himself to their subtle influences, and

144
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:39,479
then, having, as it were, caught their color and satisfied his intellectual

145
00:12:39,479 --> 00:12:43,797
curiosity, leave them with that curious indifference that is not incompatible

146
00:12:43,797 --> 00:12:48,637
with a real ardor of temperament, and that, indeed, according to certain modern

147
00:12:48,637 --> 00:12:54,119
psychologists, is often a condition of it. It was rumored of him once that he

148
00:12:54,119 --> 00:12:58,919
was about to join the Roman Catholic communion, and certainly the Roman ritual

149
00:12:58,919 --> 00:13:01,637
had always a great attraction for him.

150
00:13:01,637 --> 00:13:06,680
The daily sacrifice, more awful really than all the sacrifices of the antique

151
00:13:06,680 --> 00:13:12,360
world, stirred him as much by its superb rejection of the evidence of the senses

152
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:16,797
as by the primitive simplicity of its elements and the eternal pathos of the

153
00:13:16,797 --> 00:13:21,919
human tragedy that it sought to symbolize. He loved to kneel down on the

154
00:13:21,919 --> 00:13:27,080
cold marble pavement and watch the priest, in his stiff flowered dalmatic,

155
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:31,680
slowly and with white hands moving aside the veil of the tabernacle, or raising

156
00:13:31,680 --> 00:13:36,080
aoft the jeweled lantern-shaped monstrance with that palid wafer that at

157
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:42,479
times, one would fain think, is indeed the panis cistus the bread of angels, or,

158
00:13:42,479 --> 00:13:46,637
robed in the garments of the Passion of Christ, breaking the Host in to the

159
00:13:46,637 --> 00:13:53,040
chalice and smiting his breast for his sins. The fuming senses that the grave

160
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:58,360
boys, in their lace and scarlet, tossed into the air like great guilt flowers

161
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:03,558
had their subtle fascination for him. As he passed out, he used to look with

162
00:14:03,558 --> 00:14:08,319
wonder at the black confessionals and long to sit in the dim shadow of one of

163
00:14:08,319 --> 00:14:14,159
them and listen to men and women whispering through the worn grating the true story of their

164
00:14:14,159 --> 00:14:18,400
lives. But he never fell into the error of arresting his intellectual

165
00:14:18,400 --> 00:14:24,479
development by any formal acceptance of creed or system, or of mistaking, for a

166
00:14:24,479 --> 00:14:30,400
house in which to live, an inn that is but suitable for the sourn of a night, or

167
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:35,439
for a few hours of a night in which there are no stars and the moon is in

168
00:14:35,439 --> 00:14:40,200
travail. Mysticism, with its marvelous power of making common things strange to

169
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:45,080
us, and the subtle antinomianism that always seems to accompany it, moved him

170
00:14:45,080 --> 00:14:49,797
for a season; and for a season he inclined to the materialistic doctrines

171
00:14:49,797 --> 00:14:56,040
of the Darwinism movement in Germany, and found a curious pleasure in tracing the

172
00:14:56,040 --> 00:15:01,040
thoughts and passions of men to some pearly cell in the brain, or some white

173
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:05,558
nerve in the body, delighting in the conception of the absolute dependence of

174
00:15:05,558 --> 00:15:12,439
the spirit on certain physical conditions, morbid or healthy, normal or

175
00:15:12,439 --> 00:15:17,119
diseased. Yet, as has been said of him before, no theory of life seemed to him

176
00:15:17,119 --> 00:15:23,240
to be of any importance compared with life itself. He felt keenly conscious of how

177
00:15:23,240 --> 00:15:29,240
barren all intellectual speculation is when separated from action and

178
00:15:29,240 --> 00:15:35,797
experiment. He knew that the senses, no less than the soul, have their spiritual mysteries to

179
00:15:35,797 --> 00:15:40,159
reveal. And so he would now study perfumes and the secrets of their

180
00:15:40,159 --> 00:15:45,240
manufacturer distilling heavily scented oils and burning odorous gums from the

181
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:49,718
East. He saw that there was no mood of the mind that had not its counterpart in

182
00:15:49,718 --> 00:15:55,200
the sensuous life, and set himself to discover their true relations, wondering

183
00:15:55,200 --> 00:15:59,360
what there was in frankincense that made one mystical, and in ambergis that

184
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:05,000
stirred one’s passions, and in violets that woke the memory of dead romances,

185
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:11,000
and in musk that troubled the brain, and in shac that stained the imagination; and

186
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:15,479
seeking often to elaborate a real psychology of perfumes, and to estimate

187
00:16:15,479 --> 00:16:20,520
the several influ fluences of sweet smelling roots and scented pollen Laden

188
00:16:20,520 --> 00:16:27,439
flowers; of aromatic balms and of dark and fragrant woods; of spikenard, that

189
00:16:27,439 --> 00:16:33,319
sickens; of hovenia, that makes men mad; and of aloras that are said to be able

190
00:16:33,319 --> 00:16:39,040
to expel melancholy from the soul. At another time he devoted himself

191
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:44,400
entirely to music, and in a long laed room, with a Vermilion and gold ceiling

192
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:48,718
and walls of olive green lacquer, he used to give curious concerts in

193
00:16:48,718 --> 00:16:54,240
which mad gypsies tore wild music from little zithers, or grave yellow Shaw

194
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:58,919
Tunisians plucked at the strained strings of monstrous loots while

195
00:16:58,919 --> 00:17:04,159
grinning Negroes beat monotonously upon copper drums and, crouching upon scarlet

196
00:17:04,159 --> 00:17:10,919
mats, slim turbaned Indians blew through long pipes of reed or brass and charmed—

197
00:17:10,919 --> 00:17:16,878
—or faine to charm— —great hooded snakes and horrible horned

198
00:17:16,878 --> 00:17:21,759
adders. The harsh intervals and shrill discords of barbaric music stirred him

199
00:17:21,759 --> 00:17:27,240
at times when Schubert’s grace, and Chan's beautiful sorrows, and the mighty

200
00:17:27,240 --> 00:17:33,759
harmonies of bethoven himself, fell unheeded on his ear. He collected together from all parts

201
00:17:33,759 --> 00:17:38,718
of the world the strangest instruments that could be found, either in the tombs

202
00:17:38,718 --> 00:17:44,479
of dead nations or among the few savage tribes that have survived contact with Western

203
00:17:44,479 --> 00:17:50,558
civilizations, and love to touch and try them. He had the mysterious jurupari of

204
00:17:50,558 --> 00:17:55,159
the Rio Negro Indians, that women are not allowed to look at and that even youths

205
00:17:55,159 --> 00:17:59,878
may not see till they have been subjected to fasting and scourging, and

206
00:17:59,878 --> 00:18:05,360
the earn jars of the Peruvians that have the shrill cries of birds, and fluts of

207
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:11,637
human bones such as Alfonso de oal heard in Chile, and the sonorous green jaspers

208
00:18:11,637 --> 00:18:17,040
that are found near Cusco and give forth a note of singular sweetness.

